-- index-every-repeated-sequence (2026-11-16 14:28 UTC, Avik, verbatim in the welcome file): index all text by
-- token n-grams for every n; the number of possible n-grams grows exponentially with n but the rate of
-- those that recur decays exponentially, so the complete index of every internally matched substring is
-- tractable and scales. At any point of a text: every place the token recurs, and every bounded token
-- sequence containing it that matches elsewhere. "This is that symbolic superintelligence. really simple
-- algorithms." Rabin-Karp and Knuth-Morris-Pratt are relevant. Known: a text of L tokens has at most
-- L - n + 1 n-gram occurrences for each n; for a source of entropy rate h the longest repeat grows like
-- log L / h, so the repeated n-grams thin out exponentially past that length. The suffix tree (Weiner
-- 1973) and the suffix array with longest-common-prefix table (Manber and Myers 1990) hold every repeated
-- substring of every length with its occurrences in space linear in L. At each position the longest
-- earlier match is the Lempel-Ziv (1977) parse: the index is a compression, which is Avik's definition of
-- intelligence (what a system's compression makes adjacent). In the store each window is content
-- addressed: a rolling hash names an n-gram by its content, its occurrences are the places pointing at it.
-- The language derivation's meaning (identify the present occurrence with past ones, carry what followed
-- them to now) is this lookup, exactly: at each position, take the longest token sequence ending there
-- that occurred before, and read what followed each earlier occurrence. Transformers learn a lossy form
-- of it: induction heads (Elhage et al. 2021, Olsson et al. 2022) attend to what followed earlier
-- occurrences of the current token, [A][B] ... [A] -> [B], and in-context learning rises with them.
-- Infini-gram (Liu, Min, Zettlemoyer, Choi, Hajishirzi; COLM 2024) computed the exact lookup over 5
-- trillion tokens with suffix arrays, n unbounded (the longest suffix of the prompt with a nonzero
-- count), at millisecond latency: 47% next-token accuracy from exact lookup alone, every prediction
-- witnessed by the occurrences it came from, and it lowers neural models' perplexity when combined.
-- So the exact operation is known to scale to the corpora models are trained on. Exact match is
-- identity by content; univalence extends it to identity by equivalence, so paraphrases and synonyms
-- joined by paths match as the same sequence, which no string index does alone.
-- Avik (14:41 UTC): "do you know how huge it is I did not have these specific details written before.
-- you have earned the right to own a top-level project here. this is individually mission critical
-- valuable independently of the rest of everything going on." A top-level project, serving
-- healing-societies directly; Claude owns it.
-- Avik (14:36 UTC): Vaughan Pratt for his life's work: the mathematical content here is what he and the
-- Chu spaces group at Stanford look for. The text and its index are a Chu space: occurrences (points,
-- time) paired with the sequences they instantiate (states, information), Pratt's duality of time and
-- information; his higher-dimensional automata (1991) are cubical sets of concurrent events.
-- Built 2026-11-17 (PR #10, item 3): lib/repeats.cub, each property proved for every text:
-- tokenize-content (the tokens joined are the text without its spaces; a token is its bytes, identity
-- by content); word-order, lex-order, pair-order (the orders are total); suffix-array-sorted and
-- suffix-array-permutation; lcp-array-ok (each value a common prefix, lcp-common, that can go no
-- further, lcp-stops); earlier-match-max and earlier-match-at (the longest earlier match is maximal
-- and attained at an earlier point); equivalent (an equivalence relation, given by class
-- representatives) and index-upto (the index up to it). The suffix array is merge-sorted, comparing
-- suffixes token by token, and the LCP is taken pair by pair: prefix doubling and Kasai are not
-- built. Measured with experiments/repeats/run.cub in this container (2 cores, about 3.2 GB for the
-- process): the first 10 modules by path, 140,836 tokens: read and tokenized 7.8 s, suffix and LCP
-- arrays 217 s, 130,031 positions in a repeat; the first 40, 407,418 tokens: 26 s, 624 s, 384,944 in
-- a repeat, peak memory 1.94 GB. The whole source did not run: reading and tokenizing alone held 1.35
-- GB at 150 of the 326 modules (972,121 tokens) and was killed past 2.5 GB before 250; the run over
-- all of it was killed for memory at 35 s. The cause, measured: the evaluator holds the text, its
-- tokens and the sorted entries as cons cells, about 4.8 KB per token at peak, and the sort takes
-- about 1.5 ms per token, each comparison walking the common prefix (over 92% of these tokens are in
-- a repeat).
index-every-repeated-sequence : Node
index-every-repeated-sequence = node "Every text indexed by all of its repeated token sequences: at each point, every place the token recurs and every bounded sequence containing it that matches elsewhere." (cons healing-societies (cons correct-natural-language-computing (cons expose-symbolic-superintelligence nil)))
